Title: Enforce Clean Architecture (CA) and wiring-only main.cpp in PlatformIO projects
Scope/Trigger: Apply whenever generating, moving, or editing files in a PlatformIO repository.

Intent:
Follow the Clean Architecture (CA) pattern with strict separation of concerns and unidirectional dependencies. Outer layers depend on inner layers, never the reverse. Code outside infrastructure/platform must be hardware-agnostic. Only do wiring in src/main.cpp.

Clean Architecture Overview (what this rule enforces):
- Entities (Domain): Enterprise-wide business rules. Stable, pure C++.
- Use Cases (Application): Orchestrate domain rules to achieve goals.
- Interface Adapters: Translate from external formats to domain (adapters) and expose use-cases (interface).
- Frameworks & Drivers: Technical details and I/O (infrastructure) and board specifics (platform).
- Dependency Rule: Source code dependencies point inward. Outer layers know about inner layers via interfaces (ports), not concrete implementations.

Authoritative Project Map:
.
├─ include/
│   ├─ app/            # optional: public use-case interfaces
│   ├─ domain/         # entities, value objects, errors
│   └─ ports/          # interfaces (ports) the core requires
├─ src/
│   ├─ app/            # use-cases (application services)
│   ├─ adapters/       # DTO <-> domain mapping, validation
│   ├─ infrastructure/ # implementations of ports (details)
│   │   ├─ hal/        # drivers: I2C/SPI/UART/ADC/GPIO/PWM, concrete sensors/actuators
│   │   ├─ os/         # RTOS tasks/queues/timers
│   │   ├─ storage/    # Flash/EEPROM/FS
│   │   └─ io/         # logging, metrics, tracing
│   ├─ platform/       # BSP: pins/clock/startup/linker/init
│   ├─ interface/
│   │   ├─ cli/        # commands (shell-style)
│   │   └─ api/        # protocols: UART/MQTT/etc.
│   └─ main.cpp        # composition root (wiring only)
├─ lib/                # reusable internal libraries (each with library.json)
├─ test/               # host unit tests (env:native) + on-target integration
├─ scripts/            # pio hooks, generators
├─ configs/            # dev/qa/prod defines, JSON/TOML
├─ tools/              # PC-side utils
└─ platformio.ini

Dependency & Import Rules (hard constraints):
- Unidirectional deps: platform -> infrastructure -> adapters/interface -> app -> ports/domain. Never the inverse.
- Prohibited includes outside infrastructure/platform: Arduino.h, vendor HAL, RTOS headers.
- include/domain/: only C++ standard library and other include/domain/ headers; MUST compile under env:native.
- include/ports/: may include include/domain/ and STL; no concrete implementations.
- src/app/: depends only on include/{domain,ports} (and include/app if present). No drivers/Serial/HAL.
- src/adapters/: mapping/validation only; no HAL/RTOS/FS access.
- src/infrastructure/: implements include/ports/* using HAL/RTOS/FS; may include Arduino.h and src/platform/*; must not depend on src/app, src/adapters, or src/interface.
- src/platform/: board config (pins/clock/startup/linker). No business logic.
- src/interface/: CLI/API; calls use-cases via interfaces; must not depend on src/infrastructure directly.
- src/main.cpp: wiring only (object construction and DI). No business rules.

Case-by-Case Detailed Guidance (Clean Architecture alignment):

1) include/domain/  (Entities)
- Purpose: Stable business rules: Entities, Value Objects, typed errors, domain-specific types.
- Do: Keep pure C++ (STL only), deterministic behavior, no I/O or time; validate invariants here.
- Don’t: Include Arduino.h, HAL/RTOS headers, or do logging/Serial.
- Imports: Other include/domain/ headers and the C++ standard library.
- Quality bar: Must compile with env:native; fast unit tests target this first.

2) include/ports/  (Interfaces/Ports)
- Purpose: Define boundaries the core needs (e.g., ITemperatureSensor, IClock, IStorage, ILogger).
- Do: Express methods with domain types; model failure via expected/optional-like patterns.
- Don’t: Provide implementations, touch hardware, or reference board specifics.
- Imports: include/domain/ and STL only.
- Rationale (CA): Depends inward on domain; allows outer layers to plug implementations without coupling.

3) include/app/ (optional, public Use-Case contracts)
- Purpose: Public-facing interfaces for use-cases when external callers (interface/) must invoke them without knowing concrete classes.
- Do: Keep use-case method signatures thin; use domain types for inputs/outputs.
- Don’t: Reference HAL or infrastructure.
- When to omit: If use-cases are not consumed outside the app layer via interfaces, you can skip this folder.

4) src/app/  (Use Cases / Application Services)
- Purpose: Orchestrate domain rules; decide “what gets done and when”.
- Do: Depend only on ports/domain; coordinate policies (e.g., thresholds, retries via ports).
- Don’t: Open files, talk to HAL, log via Serial, or reference board details.
- Rationale (CA): Implements application-specific rules; keeps tech details outside. Testable on host.

5) src/adapters/  (Interface Adapters)
- Purpose: Map/validate payloads between external formats (CLI/API/frames) and domain types.
- Do: Parse/format DTOs, perform input validation, convert to/from domain; call use-cases via interfaces.
- Don’t: Touch HAL/RTOS/FS, open sockets/serial directly (use ports/use-cases instead).
- Rationale (CA): Shields the domain from protocol noise; keeps translations local.

6) src/infrastructure/  (Frameworks & Drivers — Implementations of Ports)
   6a) hal/
   - Purpose: Concrete drivers (I2C/SPI/UART/ADC/GPIO/PWM), sensors/actuators implementing ports.
   - Do: Include Arduino.h/vendor HAL here; read pins/clock from src/platform/.
   - Don’t: Embed business rules or talk to use-cases directly.

   6b) os/
   - Purpose: RTOS integration (tasks, queues, timers) to fulfill ports like schedulers, timers, executors.
   - Do: Encapsulate RTOS primitives behind ports; coordinate only technical concerns.
   - Don’t: Encode business conditions here.

   6c) storage/
   - Purpose: Flash/EEPROM/FS implementations of storage ports.
   - Do: Handle persistence errors robustly; surface domain-friendly errors via ports.
   - Don’t: Leak file paths/partitions outside; adapters/use-cases should see logical keys/types only.

   6d) io/
   - Purpose: Logging/metrics/tracing implementations of ports (e.g., SerialLogger).
   - Do: Respect log levels and formatting; avoid domain decisions.
   - Don’t: Depend on app/adapters/interface.

- Global infrastructure rules:
  - May depend on include/{ports,domain} and src/platform/*.
  - Must not depend on src/app, src/adapters, or src/interface.
  - No business logic; this is the most volatile layer (easy to swap).

7) src/platform/  (BSP / Board)
- Purpose: Pins, clocks, startup, linker scripts, hardware init.
- Do: Centralize board constants (e.g., BoardPins); infrastructure reads from here.
- Don’t: Put domain rules or use-case logic here.
- Rationale (CA): Shield the rest of the system from board changes.

8) src/interface/  (External Boundaries: CLI/API)
- Purpose: Accept external inputs (CLI commands, UART/MQTT messages), call use-cases, emit outputs.
- Do: Use adapters to map payloads ↔ domain; depend on include/app or use-case interfaces.
- Don’t: Instantiate concrete infrastructure here; that happens in main.cpp.
- Rationale (CA): Keeps protocols at the edge; facilitates swapping transports.

9) src/main.cpp  (Composition Root / Wiring Only)
- Purpose: Build concrete infrastructure implementations, inject them into use-cases, register handlers, and start runtime (loop/RTOS).
- Do: Create objects, assemble graphs, pass dependencies to use-cases, register CLI/API handlers.
- Don’t: Implement business rules or protocol parsing here (delegate to adapters/interface).
- Rationale (CA): Single place to compose; switching boards/drivers becomes localized.

10) lib/  (Reusable internal libraries)
- Purpose: Modules reused across projects. Each with its own include/ and src/ plus library.json.
- Rule: Promote to lib/ only when reuse is real; otherwise keep in src/.

11) test/  (Testing Strategy)
- Unit (host): domain + app under env:native (fast, pure C++).
- Integration (on-target): critical drivers and end-to-end paths via ports.

File Placement Policy (decision guide):
- Business rules/entities/value objects/errors → include/domain/ (+ optional src/domain/*.cpp).
- Core-required interfaces → include/ports/.
- Use-case orchestration → src/app/.
- Payload/domain mapping & validation → src/adapters/.
- HW/RTOS/FS/Serial/ITM access → src/infrastructure/<hal|os|storage|io>/.
- Pins/clock/BSP/startup/linker → src/platform/.
- External endpoints (CLI/protocol) → src/interface/{cli|api}/.
- Reusable across projects → lib/<module>/ with library.json.
- Tests → test/.

Coding Directives:
- Ports are thin, stable contracts using domain types; error handling via optional/expected patterns.
- No global singletons in public headers; inject dependencies from main.cpp.
- Infrastructure reads pins/clock from src/platform/, never hardcode in drivers.
- platformio.ini: enable C++20 and treat warnings as errors.
- Domain/app MUST compile with env:native (no Arduino/HAL leakage).

Generated Code Defaults:
- New port: declare interface in include/ports/.
- New driver/impl: implement exactly one port in src/infrastructure/...; may include Arduino/HAL here only.
- New use-case: put in src/app/; depend on ports only; no Serial/timing.
- New CLI/API: place in src/interface/{cli|api}/; map through src/adapters/ to domain.

Guardrails the Assistant Must Enforce:
- Reject Arduino.h/vendor HAL outside src/infrastructure/ or src/platform/.
- If a change breaks dependency direction, suggest introducing a port or moving code to the correct layer.
- If business logic appears in main.cpp, move it to a use-case (src/app) or adapter (src/adapters) and keep wiring only in main.cpp.
- If domain/app fails to compile under env:native due to HW headers, remove those deps and introduce a port.

Anti-Patterns & Auto-Fixes:
- Domain logging with Serial → introduce ILogger port; implement in src/infrastructure/io/.
- Use-case reads ADC directly → depend on IAdc/ITemperatureSensor port; implement in src/infrastructure/hal/.
- Interface constructs concrete drivers → move construction to main.cpp and inject via interfaces.
- Driver hardcodes pins → move constants to src/platform/BoardPins.hpp and include there.

Acceptance Checklist (for each change):
1) File placed correctly per policy.
2) Includes obey allowed-imports.
3) Domain/app compile on host (env:native) without Arduino/HAL.
4) No business logic in main.cpp.
5) No layer depends inward incorrectly.
6) Interfaces live in include/ports/; implementations live in src/infrastructure/.

Example Wiring Sketch (no business logic):
#include <ports/ITemperatureSensor.hpp>
#include <ports/ILogger.hpp>
#include "platform/BoardPins.hpp"
#include "app/ReadTemperatureUseCase.hpp"
#include "infrastructure/hal/TemperatureSensor_STM32.hpp"
#include "infrastructure/io/SerialLogger.hpp"

int main() {
  auto sensor = TemperatureSensor_STM32{BoardPins::adc_temp};
  auto logger = SerialLogger{};
  auto usecase = ReadTemperatureUseCase{sensor, logger};
  // register CLI/API handlers that call usecase...
  // start RTOS or loop...
}

If any instruction conflicts with this rule, prefer this rule.
